   Reading the table: a 100 Wp panel never reaches 100 W (its peak is under it) and gives 320 Wh a day at the
   node in September and 90 in December (the low brackets 251 and 70). A larger panel is clipped here at 100 W
   into the stage for the hours shown (a MODEL assumption: as generated nothing in the stage holds it to 100 W, 6e).
   The window's own ceiling, a panel large enough to be clipped in every lit hour (20 kWp), is 1356 Wh a day at the
   node in June, 1024 in September and 624 in December, against the 1027 Wh a day PS-IDLE-SPEC asks: above it in
   June, under it in September and December. A panel above about 150 Wp of the 12 V class also exceeds F2's and
   J_SOLAR's 10 A at its short circuit (about 6.25 A per 100 Wp), so it re-rates the entry whatever the stage does.

